# React components

## Client components

- ALL components in this project are client components. Start every `.tsx` file with the `'use client'` directive on the first line (before imports).
- Do not create server components, even if the task looks static.

## Component folder structure

Each component lives in its own folder named after the component (PascalCase):

ComponentName/
├── ComponentName.tsx
├── ComponentName.module.css
├── ComponentName.test.tsx
├── assets/                    # Static only: images, SVG, fonts, JSON
└── ...                        # Subcomponents/hooks if used only by this component

- Styles (`.module.css`) and tests (`.test.tsx`) sit next to the component file, NOT in `assets/`.
- Static assets (images, icons, fonts, JSON) go in `assets/` inside the component folder.
- Import static assets as `./assets/icon.svg`.
- Subcomponents used only by this component may live in the same folder (e.g. `ComponentName/SubComponent.tsx`).

## Component file structure

1. `'use client'`
2. Imports (React, Next.js, MobX, styles, static assets, other components).
3. Props interface: `interface [ComponentName]Props { ... }` (above the component).
4. Component function: `export function ComponentName({ ... }: ComponentNameProps) { ... }`.
5. If the component uses a MobX store, wrap it in `observer` from `mobx-react-lite` and use the `useStore` hook from `@/stores/useStore`.

## Size

- A component file must not exceed 200 lines. If it grows larger, extract subcomponents into separate files in the same folder.
- If the component has complex logic (more than 2–3 `useState`/`useEffect` hooks), move it into a custom hook in a separate `use[Name].ts` file in the same folder.

## Styles

- Each component uses its own CSS Module: `import styles from './ComponentName.module.css'`.
- Use camelCase class names in CSS (e.g. `.userProfile`) so you can access them as `styles.userProfile`.
